26.1  Reading the journal with journalctl

TL;DR
  - systemd's journal collects everything: the kernel, every service's output,
    and programs that log through syslog. One command reads it.
  - Narrow it down: by boot (-b), unit (-u), priority (-p), time (--since), text
    (-g). -f follows new lines as they come.
  - Reading other users' and system logs needs sudo, or membership in adm or
    systemd-journal.

      journalctl -b -p warning              # warnings and worse, this boot
      journalctl -u nginx --since "1 hour ago"
      journalctl -f -u myapp                # follow one service
      journalctl -b -1 -e                   # end of the previous boot

On your machine
  Journal   on disk (/var/log/journal): kept across reboots · 300 B in 2 files
  Settings  SystemMaxUse=500M
  Syslog    rsyslog also writes text files in /var/log
  Rotation  2 rules in /etc/logrotate.d
  Biggest   /var/log/syslog 4.9 KiB · /var/log/nginx/access.log 2.9 KiB ·
            /var/log/auth.log 1.2 KiB

Pitfalls
  - -b -1 shows nothing: the journal is kept in RAM only, so earlier boots are
    gone (26.2).
  - Priorities count down: -p err means error and worse (crit, alert, emerg),
    not just errors.
  - --since takes local time; --utc shows and parses times in UTC.
  - Output goes through a pager: --no-pager for scripts, -o json to parse it.

Deep dive

  ▾ What is stored besides the message?

    Each entry carries fields: _SYSTEMD_UNIT, _PID, _UID, _COMM, PRIORITY, the
    boot ID and more, set by journald itself so programs can't fake them. Any
    field filters: journalctl _UID=1000, journalctl _COMM=sshd.
    journalctl -o verbose shows them all.

  ▾ Why a binary format?

    Indexed by field and time, so filtering a month of logs by unit is instant,
    and entries can be checked for tampering (--verify). The cost: you need
    journalctl to read them, not grep on a file.

Related
  Commands  journalctl
  Paths     /var/log/journal/
  Hub       idk logs Logs
  See also  idk 26.2 Where logs go: journald, syslog and /var/log
  See also  idk 22.2 Why a service won't start
  See also  idk 21.3 dmesg and the kernel log
